> Roguelikes are difficult to internationalize the right way,
> with separately distributed locale data and a message
> catalogue for the translation, because they assemble bits and
> pieces of text into complete sentences in a variety of ways.
> I know of no roguelike that is properly internationalized.

Just for the record, the problem isn't particular to roguelikes;
it affects any program that wants to do anything but the
simplest output in the form of sentences (as opposed to e.g.
tables).  In practice, you almost always need a dynamically
loaded object to handle the messages, rewriting the actual code
for each message.

The problem is a little easier if you restrict yourself to
western European languages, but even there, each of the
languages has its odd little quirks.  (In French, for example,
getting the agreement correct for the past participle in a
compound past.)

